WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.770
In the last video, you saw how your DEV and test sets should come from the same distribution.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.450
But how large should they be?

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.210
The guidelines for how to set up your DEV and test sets are changing in the deep learning

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.210
era.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.450
Let's take a look at some best practices.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.770
You might have heard of the rule of thumb in machine learning of taking all the data

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:26.850
you have and using a 70-30 split into a train and test set.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:37.170
If you had to set up train, DEV and test sets, maybe you would use 60% training and say 20%

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:44.640
DEV and 20% tests.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:52.620
In earlier eras of machine learning, this was pretty reasonable, especially back when data

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.700
set sizes were just smaller.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:02.860
So if you had 100 examples in total, these 70-30 or 60-20-20 rule of thumb would be pretty

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:03.860
reasonable.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:12.020
If you had 1,000 examples, maybe if you had 10,000 examples, these heuristics are not unreasonable.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:19.150
But in the modern machine learning era, we're now used to working with much larger data

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.470
set sizes.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:27.020
So let's say you have a million training examples.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.900
It might be quite reasonable to set up your data so that you have 98% in the training

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:41.140
set and 1% DEV and 1% test.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.220
I'm going to use DNT to re-viate the DEV and test sets.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.940
Because if you have a million examples, then 1% of that is 10,000 examples and that might

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.100
be plenty enough for a DEV set or for a test set.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:00.460
So in the modern deep learning era, where sometimes we have much larger data sets, it's

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.940
quite reasonable to use a much smaller than 20% or 30% of your data for a DEV set or a

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:07.940
test set.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.740
And because deep learning algorithms have such a huge hunger for data, I'm seeing that for

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:18.660
problems where you have large data sets that a much larger fraction of it goes into the

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.340
training set.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.530
So how about the test set?

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:31.370
Remember the purpose of your test set is that after you finish developing a system, the test

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.050
set helps you evaluate how good your final system is.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.770
So the guideline is to set your test set to be big enough to give high confidence in the

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.250
overall performance of your system.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:47.090
So unless you need to have a very accurate measure of how well your final system is

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:54.140
performing, maybe you don't need millions and millions of examples in your test set.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.900
And maybe for your application, if you think that having 10,000 examples gives you enough

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.260
confidence in the final performance or maybe 100,000 or whatever it is, that might be

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.740
enough. And this could be much less than say 30% of your overall data set, depending on

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.870
how much data you have.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.310
For some applications, maybe you don't need a high confidence in the overall

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.230
performance of your final system.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.190
Maybe all you need is a train and DEV set.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:28.510
And I think not having a test set might be OK.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:37.190
In fact, what sometimes happened was people were talking about using train test splits,

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.550
but what they were actually doing was iterating on the test set.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:46.550
So rather than a test set, what they had was a train, DEV split and no test set.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.910
If you're actually tuning to this set, to this DEV set or this test set, it's better to

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.330
call the DEV set.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.850
Although I think in the history of machine learning, not everyone has been completely clean

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:03.930
and completely rigorous about calling the DEV set when it really should be treated as a DEV set.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.610
But if all you care about is having some data to train on and having some data to tune to,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:14.410
and you're just going to ship the final system regardless and not worry too much about how

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.210
it's actually doing, I think it'd be healthier to just call the train DEV set and

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.850
acknowledge that you have no test set.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.690
This is a bit unusual.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.850
I'm definitely not recommending not having a test set.

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:30.810
When I'm building a system, I do find it reassuring to have a separate test set you can use to

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.290
get an unbiased estimate of how it was doing before you ship it.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:40.450
But if you have a very large DEV set so that you think you won't overfit the DEV set

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:45.370
too badly, maybe it's not totally unreasonable to just have a train DEV set.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.930
Although it's not what I usually recommend.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:54.450
So to summarize, in the era of big data, I think the old rule of thumb of a 70-30

00:04:54.450 --> 00:05:00.130
that no longer applies and the trend has been to use more data for training and less for

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.410
DEV and test, especially when you're very large data sets.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:07.050
And the rule of thumb is really to try to set the DEV set big enough for its purpose,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.410
which is help you evaluate different ideas and pick an algorithm A or B better.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.010
And the test set, the purpose of the test set is to help you evaluate your final class

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.010
files.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.570
You just have to set your test set big enough for that purpose and that could be much less

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.810
than 30% of your data.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.170
So I hope that gives some guidance and some suggestions on how to set up your DEV and

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:28.570
test sets in the deep learning era.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.890
Next, it turns out that sometimes, partway through a machine learning problem, you might

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.890
want to change your evaluation metric or change your DEV and test sets.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.610
Let's talk about when you might want to do that.
